WEBVTT

00:00:01.50 --> 00:00:07.12
This is journey thirty three of Hewn, the software factory: use Mission Control on

00:00:07.12 --> 00:00:07.60
mobile.

00:00:07.60 --> 00:00:13.22
An Owner checking on a direction from a phone or a tablet needs every page to work

00:00:13.22 --> 00:00:13.90
there too.

00:00:13.90 --> 00:00:18.56
This video shows the same pages at phone, tablet and desktop widths,

00:00:18.56 --> 00:00:20.21
and the new menu drawer.

00:00:21.36 --> 00:00:26.46
Every page works from three hundred and twenty to two thousand five hundred and

00:00:26.46 --> 00:00:27.62
sixty pixels wide.

00:00:27.62 --> 00:00:31.69
On a phone, content is one column, navigation is a menu drawer,

00:00:31.69 --> 00:00:34.02
and the pipeline is a vertical list.

00:00:34.02 --> 00:00:39.05
On a tablet, the pipeline becomes a scrollable stepper above the stage detail.

00:00:39.05 --> 00:00:43.64
From nine hundred and ninety two pixels, navigation is a fixed sidebar,

00:00:43.64 --> 00:00:45.96
with the pipeline beside the detail.

00:00:45.96 --> 00:00:47.90
And no width scrolls sideways.

00:00:49.05 --> 00:00:51.50
Here is one project at three widths.

00:00:51.50 --> 00:00:54.84
On the desktop, the sidebar is fixed on the left,

00:00:54.84 --> 00:00:57.98
and the pipeline sits beside the stage detail.

00:00:57.98 --> 00:01:03.57
On a tablet, the sidebar goes, and the eleven stages become a stepper that scrolls

00:01:03.57 --> 00:01:05.41
sideways, above the detail.

00:01:05.41 --> 00:01:10.04
On a phone, the stages stack into a list, and the detail follows it.

00:01:11.19 --> 00:01:15.31
Before this journey, a phone showed only the wordmark at the top.

00:01:15.31 --> 00:01:17.78
There was no way to reach another page.

00:01:17.78 --> 00:01:22.28
Now the top bar has a menu button, the wordmark and the connection dot.

00:01:22.63 --> 00:01:24.21
The Owner taps the menu.

00:01:24.21 --> 00:01:29.45
The drawer slides in, with the sidebar's own links, the workers and the account.

00:01:29.45 --> 00:01:32.80
Focus starts on Close, and stays inside the drawer.

00:01:32.80 --> 00:01:36.54
Escape closes it, and focus goes back to the menu button.

00:01:36.54 --> 00:01:39.36
The Owner opens it again and taps Projects.

00:01:39.36 --> 00:01:42.64
The page changes, and the drawer closes by itself.

00:01:42.64 --> 00:01:46.51
Then the Owner opens a project, and its pipeline is a list.

00:01:46.86 --> 00:01:49.14
The drawer reads the same in dark mode.

00:01:50.29 --> 00:01:54.94
The requirement asked for forty four pixel touch targets at every width.

00:01:54.94 --> 00:01:59.98
But the design system draws thirty two and forty pixel buttons on the desktop,

00:01:59.98 --> 00:02:01.85
where a mouse is the pointer.

00:02:01.85 --> 00:02:07.28
So the requirement now asks for forty four pixels below nine hundred and ninety two,

00:02:07.28 --> 00:02:11.02
and twenty four, the accessibility minimum, from there up.

00:02:11.02 --> 00:02:13.29
A new decision record explains why.

00:02:13.64 --> 00:02:19.19
On a phone, small and medium buttons, fields and tabs are now forty four pixels.

00:02:19.19 --> 00:02:23.43
The mocks read the same tokens, so they changed with the app,

00:02:23.43 --> 00:02:25.03
and no mock was edited.

00:02:26.18 --> 00:02:29.93
The shell keeps the sidebar's content in one template.

00:02:29.93 --> 00:02:34.58
From nine hundred and ninety two pixels it fills the fixed sidebar.

00:02:34.58 --> 00:02:40.00
Below that, the menu button opens the same template in a dialog labelled Menu.

00:02:40.00 --> 00:02:45.48
Navigating closes it, and so does widening the window, through one media query.

00:02:45.83 --> 00:02:50.09
The tokens set small and medium controls to forty four pixels,

00:02:50.09 --> 00:02:54.14
and restore thirty two and forty from the large breakpoint.

00:02:54.14 --> 00:02:59.29
The rest of the layout was already in the design system's CSS: the columns,

00:02:59.29 --> 00:03:01.49
and the pipeline's three shapes.

00:03:01.84 --> 00:03:05.11
The class diagram shows the shell owning the drawer.

00:03:05.11 --> 00:03:09.95
There's no breakpoint service any more, so the band never reaches TypeScript.

00:03:11.10 --> 00:03:16.04
A behaviour test opens the drawer at three hundred and seventy five pixels.

00:03:16.04 --> 00:03:19.59
It checks that focus starts on Close and stays inside,

00:03:19.59 --> 00:03:23.68
then closes the drawer with Escape, with Close, by navigating,

00:03:23.68 --> 00:03:25.32
and with a tap beside it.

00:03:25.32 --> 00:03:29.40
Before the change it failed, because there was no menu button.

00:03:29.75 --> 00:03:32.66
The sweep visits every page at seven widths,

00:03:32.66 --> 00:03:37.21
from three hundred and twenty to two thousand five hundred and sixty.

00:03:37.21 --> 00:03:41.31
That's ninety one pages and widths, and none scrolls sideways.

00:03:41.31 --> 00:03:45.07
Another test measures the pipeline's shape at each width.

00:03:45.42 --> 00:03:48.83
The touch target test measures every visible control.

00:03:48.83 --> 00:03:53.59
At first it found sixty six under forty four pixels on phones and tablets.

00:03:53.59 --> 00:03:58.34
After the token change, it found tabs squeezed narrower than their labels,

00:03:58.34 --> 00:04:01.94
so each tab now keeps its width, and then it found none.

00:04:01.94 --> 00:04:06.12
Visual tests compare the top bar and the drawer with their mocks,

00:04:06.12 --> 00:04:09.27
and an accessibility test checks the open drawer.

00:04:10.42 --> 00:04:12.57
Journey thirty three is complete.

00:04:12.57 --> 00:04:17.80
Phones and tablets get a menu drawer, and every page fits from three hundred and

00:04:17.80 --> 00:04:21.25
twenty to two thousand five hundred and sixty pixels.

00:04:21.25 --> 00:04:25.50
The pipeline is a list, a stepper, or a column beside the detail.

00:04:25.50 --> 00:04:28.43
And below nine hundred and ninety two pixels,

00:04:28.43 --> 00:04:31.63
every control is a forty four pixel touch target.

00:04:31.63 --> 00:04:33.65
Next comes journey thirty four.
